cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code, please cite the article below and the software itself."
type: software
title: "Can SEAS5 seasonal forecasts drive daily streamflow prediction? Machine learning experiments in Central Asian catchments"
version: 1.0.0
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  Code for a study testing whether ECMWF's openly available SEAS5 seasonal
  forecast carries usable information for daily discharge prediction at lead
  times up to 214 days, in seven Central Asian and two European Alpine
  catchments. Ridge regression, Random Forest, XGBoost and an LSTM network
  are trained to predict the log-transformed discharge anomaly relative to the
  day-of-year climatology and evaluated by leave-one-year-out
  cross-validation against climatology, persistence and damped-persistence
  baselines: untuned, after nested-CV tuning, and under corrupted forcing.
  The repository covers the SEAS5 download and preprocessing, the three
  experimental stages, and the scripts that reproduce the paper's figures.
keywords:
  - Seasonal streamflow forecasting
  - Central Asia
  - SEAS5
  - Machine learning
  - Forecast verification
  - Snow-dominated catchments
